## What it actually is

A toy-scale empirical demonstration — **no neural networks trained** — that you can do constraint-conditioned generation by running diffusion-style transport over Hyperdimensional Computing space, using an analytically constructed score function from an ASP-style constraint program. Domain is *Cloak of Darkness*, the canonical interactive-fiction example.

## The headline numbers (from the README)
| Experiment | Result |
|----|----|
| Exp 1 — slot recovery | 100% across all 270 Cloak states at D=10000 |
| Exp 2 — mean-field score | \~20% SAT (baseline; better than 8% random) |
| Exp 3 — corrective score | **97–100% SAT, mean 3 steps** from random init |
| Exp 4 — noisy-input refinement | 100% on single-violation, fails on triple |
| Exp 5 — annealing | Bimodal: T=0 → 100% (1 mode), T₀=3 → 95% (many modes); light noise T₀=0.3 → **0%** |
| Exp 6 — mode diversity | 24/29 distinct SAT configs in 50 runs |


The interesting structural claim is in Exp 3: the score function is constructed analytically (`find_nearest_fix` over enumerated SAT states), so this is meant as an existence proof that the diffusion machinery does the work — the network isn't where the magic lives. The README is explicit that this is a discovery prototype (\~600 lines, reads end-to-end), with Phase A (LLM-as-translator) and Phase B (learned MLP score net) flagged as the actual research bets.

