I poked around. Short version: **the moving-shapes-with-hidden-course direction is essentially untrodden territory** in mainstream Battleship variants. Here's the lay of the land.

### What exists (mostly orthogonal to your design)

**Hex grid is already done — but ships are still static.** In 2008, an updated version of Battleship was released using hexagonal tiles, and each player's board contains several islands on which "captured man" figurines can be placed. Ships may be placed only around the islands, and only in the player's half of the board. So Hasbro took the hex step but kept everything else classic. Interestingly, analysts found the 2008 split-board layout problematic — there's a [Possibly Wrong post](https://possiblywrong.wordpress.com/2016/03/12/analysis-of-hexagonal-battleship/) arguing it's combinatorially unfair vs. the 10×10 original. **Useful prior for your R6/R11: pick a hex topology that doesn't split the board into halves.**

**"Move a ship" rules exist but are anemic.** Two flavors show up repeatedly:

1. A variant allows a player to move one of their ships to a new, uncalled location every fourth or fifth move — i.e., a teleport-style relocation, not continuous motion. Kills the inference loop you want.
2. A more interesting variant where ships may move in orthogonal directions, but the type of ship, distance, and direction of the move must be announced to one's opponent, and ships may only fire on neighbouring squares, including diagonals, but a near-miss must be declared as such. This is *closer* to what you're after — but it **announces the move**, which violates your R9 (preserve imperfect info).

Neither one does what you're proposing: **continuous, hidden, damage-coupled motion with footprint-style information persistence.**

**Information-obfuscation variants exist** (and they're the sophisticated branch of the family tree):

* Salvo / Indian variant: instead of announcing whether a shot is a hit or miss immediately, the players are only required to announce how many of their opponent's three shots were hits, and on which kind of vessel(s), providing more strategy in the game and moving away from players' dependence on pure luck. Your "footprint" idea is in the *same family* as this — it's all about controlling what the attacker learns per probe — but pointed in a different direction (delayed spatial info instead of aggregated hit count).
* "Ship Attack" adds probe diversity: a reconnaissance airplane reports sequences of ship parts in a row or column, a satellite image can be taken to count ship parts on a 3×3 square, and a paratrooper can determine if one tile is populated by a ship part or sea. **This is the closest published cousin to your "sonar ping" Axis-3 option.**

**Damage-modifies-capability exists, but for guns, not movement.** From an old rec.games.board thread: as ships become damaged their guns get knocked out — e.g., the Battleship's guns go down on a 3-3-2 basis, the Battlecruiser's 3-2-2, the Cruiser's 3-3. So the *idea* of "damage degrades a specific stat" has precedent — but degrading **speed** (your R10/R13) appears not to.

### What's notably absent from everything I found
| Your mechanic | Found in any variant? |
|----|----|
| Hex grid | ✅ (2008 Hasbro) |
| Moving ships with hidden course | ❌ (only "announced move" or "free teleport") |
| Damage slows movement | ❌ |
| Damage removes cells *and* slows (compound) | ❌ |
| Persistent "footprint" trail of past hits as inference signal | ❌ |
| Damage degrades cell-specific stats (engine/bow/etc.) | ❌ (only "guns degrade") |


### Takeaway

The direction you're carving out is **genuinely novel** in the published-variant landscape. The closest published cousin is the orthogonal-move-with-announcement rule, which the designers seemingly **chose to announce** because they didn't have a good way to handle the imperfect-information bookkeeping without it. Your **footprint mechanic is exactly the missing piece** that lets the move stay hidden while still giving the attacker something to chew on — the breadcrumb trail *is* the hidden-move bookkeeping, externalized as a visible artifact on the board.

> 💡 **Crackpot reframe**: classic Battleship is a *static-belief Bayesian inference puzzle* — the prior never moves, you just refine the posterior. What you're proposing is a **state-space estimation problem with hidden dynamics** — a Kalman-filter-shaped game, basically. The footprint trail is the *innovation sequence*, hits are sparse noisy observations, and damage is a control input that modifies the state-transition matrix. Nobody seems to have built this game, and I suspect it's because the underlying math wasn't in the cultural water for board-game designers in 1931 or 1967. It is now.

