**Panel A — Why despawn doesn't work.** Under "ship despawns when it exits the board," a random ship lives <sub>R+1 turns. At R=8 that's</sub> 7 turns. You need at minimum 17 turns just to *hit* every ship once. Despawn means your fleet evaporates before the attacker can find it. **Verdict: dead game.**

**Panel B — Why naive reflect doesn't work.** Note the log scale. The "try smallest deflection that stays in-board" rule causes ships to *skim along the boundary ring at 4.4× the uniform rate* while the interior gets 0.025× — a 175× ratio. The attacker would just shoot the edge ring (48 cells out of 217) and reliably hit everything. **Verdict: degenerate. R9 destroyed.**

**Panel C — Pacing is fine across cooldowns.** First hit lands in 4-10 turns for R=7-10. This is the right rhythm — fast enough to feel responsive, slow enough that the attacker has to *deduce* rather than just stumble onto a ship.

**Panel D — The density argument.** Classic Battleship runs at 17% ship-cell density (17 cells / 100 squares). With moving ships, you want *slightly lower* density because:

* The attacker needs maneuvering room to set traps along predicted trajectories
* Ships need space to move without immediately interacting with the boundary
* Damaged ships getting harder to hit (smaller footprint) means you want *more* search room

**The 8–14% density band hits R=7 (10.1%) or R=8 (7.8%).** R=6 (13.4%) is borderline-cramped; R=10 (5.1%) is too sparse.

