## The headline findings

**1. WRAP gives statistically perfect uniformity.** At R=8, the coefficient of variation of cell visit-rate is **0.003** — that's 1/3 of a percent. The expected Poisson noise floor is 0.012. **We're seeing variance below pure-randomness expectation**, because each trajectory deterministically tours the whole board. Max/min visit ratio = 1.017. No exploits. No edge. Pure homogeneity.

**2. Here's the beautiful math nobody told you about**: at R=8, **every straight-line trajectory on the hex torus visits all 217 cells before returning to start.** All 6 headings. From any starting cell. The loop length **equals the number of cells exactly**.

``` gu6d8HvfF1
heading E:  loop length = 217  (N=217)
heading NE: loop length = 217
heading NW: loop length = 217
heading W:  loop length = 217
heading SW: loop length = 217
heading SE: loop length = 217
```


That is to say: **a hex torus is a space-filling billiard table for straight lines**. The lattice arithmetic conspires so that a constant-heading walker is a single Hamiltonian cycle. This isn't true for all R (it depends on number-theoretic properties of N = 3R²+3R+1), but it's true for the radii you'd actually use. This is *exactly* what you want for hidden movement: maximum unpredictability with deterministic rules.

**3. Wrap loops are roughly 6× longer than reverse periods** (panel D). A ship under reverse comes back to its start in \~2(2R+1) turns (just a bounce). Under wrap, the same ship doesn't repeat for **all N turns**. From the attacker's point of view, the prior over "where is the ship now" never collapses to a small set the way it does under reverse.

---

## So why isn't this the obvious winner?

Three problems, ranked.

### Problem 1: The wrap isn't visually what your intuition says

This is the big one. You said "go off left, reappear on right." On a **square grid** that's a clean, intuitive identification: the east edge maps 1-to-1 to the west edge with no shift.

On a **hex grid**, that's *topologically impossible without a twist*. The reason: hex boards have 6-fold symmetry, but opposite edges don't line up by simple translation. The east edge of a radius-R hex board has R+1 cells, and so does the west edge — but to identify them, you have to **shift by R cells** along the edge. Concretely, the wrap vector I'm using is **(2R+1, -R)** — that's "east by 2R+1, then northwest by R." For R=8: a ship sailing due east off the boundary doesn't reappear at "the same height on the west edge" — it reappears **shifted 8 cells northward.**

For your "asteroids-style wraparound" mental model, this is jarring. Player intuition: *"my ship went off the east at (8, 0), it should come back at (-8, 0)."* Reality: *it comes back at (-9, 8).*

> 💡 **Mathy aside**: this is the same twist that makes the Klein-bottle and the flat-torus inequivalent in surface topology. A hex board can be made into a flat torus, but only with a Dehn twist. You cannot make a hex board into the simple identification space your intuition wants. Square grids let you off easy here.

You can hide this by **not drawing the off-board flight at all** — the ship just disappears off one edge and reappears somewhere on the opposite-ish edge — but players doing trajectory inference will be confused unless they internalize the twist.

### Problem 2: The footprint mechanic gets weird

Remember the gold X breadcrumb trail you wanted? Under reverse, the trail is bounded — it forms a back-and-forth scrape across the board, easy to visually read as a trajectory.

Under wrap, **the trail can wrap around the board too**. A ship hit at (8, 0), then later at (-7, 7), then later at (3, 6) — those are three points on a single straight line *across the torus*. To an attacker who hasn't internalized the wrap geometry, the breadcrumbs look like a teleporting ghost ship rather than a moving vessel. The signature inferential pleasure of "ah, those three dots are colinear, the ship is going NE" requires the player to do **modular arithmetic in their head**. Violates R8 (intuitive — no graph theory primer required).

### Problem 3: It dissolves spatial strategy

Under reverse, there are *places* on the board. Center cells are different from edge cells (slight occupancy bias). Ships have to actually deal with the boundary. Players can talk about "the north flank" or "patrolling the east side."

Under wrap, **the board is mathematically homogeneous** — every cell is structurally identical to every other cell. Beautiful for a physicist; bland for a game. There's no "good place to hide your ship" because all places are the same place.

---

## Verdict & recommendation
| Criterion | REVERSE | WRAP |
|----|----|----|
| Occupancy uniformity | Good (cv≈0.05) | **Perfect** (cv≈0.003) |
| Imperfect info (R9) | Good | **Excellent** (loop = N) |
| Intuitive (R8) | **Excellent** (ships bounce off shore) | Poor (twisted identification) |
| Footprint mechanic | **Clean** (bounded trail) | Confusing (wraps around) |
| Spatial strategy | **Has it** (edges vs center) | None (homogeneous) |
| Trajectory inference | **Linear, easy** | Requires modular arithmetic |
| Maps to naval metaphor | **Yes** ("you've reached shore") | No (asteroids, not battleships) |



**My recommendation stands: REVERSE.** Wrap is mathematically cleaner but **the cost to player intuition is too high** given that the whole point of your variant is "the attacker should *be able to* infer trajectories from sparse hits." If trajectory inference requires the player to mentally rotate through wrap vectors, you've lost R8.

> 💡 **Crackpot reframe**: WRAP is the physicist's answer. REVERSE is the navigator's answer. The game you're designing is a navigator's game — the naval metaphor is doing real work for player intuition. Wrap is what you'd build if the metaphor were "patrolling deep space" rather than "patrolling a sea." If you ever want a *sequel* — Sea Battle II: Outer Space — wrap is the natural move.

**One honorable middle ground worth considering**: **WRAP, but only along ONE axis.** Identify the E-W edges (the most intuitive pair) and leave the other two pairs as REVERSE. This gives you a hex cylinder — partial wrap, with reverse on the "poles." It's a real topology and avoids the worst of both problems. Want me to simulate it?


