## The yield/dodge spectrum, evaluated

Three flavors of the "smaller yields" rule, ordered by how disruptive they are to the deterministic-trajectory ideal:
| Rule | What happens | Cost to inference | My take |
|----|----|----|----|
| **Ghost ships** | Bodies overlap silently. Multi-ship cells exist but are invisible. Probe on multi-ship cell hits the top-of-stack one ship. | None | Cleanest. *Boring* given how much physical realism you crave. |
| **Yield-pause** | When two ships would occupy the same cell at tick T, the smaller defers its move by 1 turn. Heading unchanged. | Mild — attacker's speed estimates get noisy but heading inference is intact. The "cooldown" the attacker sees is the *effective* cooldown including pauses. | **My pick.** Lines stay lines; only spacing along the line wobbles. |
| **Dodge-turn** | Smaller ship deviates by 1 hex sideways for one move, then resumes original heading on a parallel track. | Heavy — trajectory is no longer a straight line, just two collinear segments with a jog. Two hits no longer collapse to a line if the jog happened between them. | Glorious chaos but the inference puzzle gets very hard. Save for v2 / "rough seas" rule. |
| **True turn-radius** | Ships have agility ratings, dodges happen continuously, paths curve. | Catastrophic to inference | Beautiful idea, wrong game. |



